Let the regular hexagon $ABCDEF$ have side length $s$. Since $PAD$ is an equilateral triangle with side length 6, we have $PA = AD = PD = 6$. Also, since $ABCDEF$ is a regular hexagon, all sides have the same length, so $AB = BC = CD = DE = EF = FA = s$. Since $AD$ is the distance between two vertices separated by two vertices, we have $AD = \sqrt{3} s$.
We are given that $AD = 6$, so $\sqrt{3} s = 6$, which means $s = \frac{6}{\sqrt{3}} = \frac{6\sqrt{3}}{3} = 2\sqrt{3}$.

The edges of the pyramid are the edges of the hexagon (6 edges) and the edges from the apex $P$ to each vertex of the hexagon (6 edges). The edges of the hexagon have length $s = 2\sqrt{3}$. The edges from the apex to each vertex are $PA$, $PB$, $PC$, $PD$, $PE$, $PF$. Since $PAD$ is an equilateral triangle, $PA = PD = 6$. Also, $PA = PB = PC = PD = PE = PF$, so all the edges from $P$ to the hexagon's vertices have length 6.

The sum of the lengths of the edges of the pyramid is $6s + 6(6) = 6(2\sqrt{3}) + 36 = 12\sqrt{3} + 36$.

Therefore, the sum of the lengths of the edges of the pyramid is $12\sqrt{3} + 36$.

\boxed{36 + 12 \sqrt{3}}
\text{ units }
