Let the vertices of the convex pentagon be $A = (-1, -1)$, $B = (-3, 4)$, $C = (1, 7)$, $D = (6, 5)$, and $E = (3, y)$. The area of the pentagon can be calculated using the Shoelace Theorem.
The Shoelace Theorem states that the area of a polygon with vertices $(x_1, y_1), (x_2, y_2), ..., (x_n, y_n)$ listed in order is
$$ Area = \frac{1}{2} \left| \sum_{i=1}^{n} x_i y_{i+1} - \sum_{i=1}^{n} x_{i+1} y_i \right| = \frac{1}{2} \left| (x_1y_2 + x_2y_3 + ... + x_{n-1}y_n + x_ny_1) - (x_2y_1 + x_3y_2 + ... + x_ny_{n-1} + x_1y_n) \right| $$
In our case, we have the vertices $(-1, -1)$, $(-3, 4)$, $(1, 7)$, $(6, 5)$, $(3, y)$.
So, the area of the pentagon is:
$$ Area = \frac{1}{2} \left| (-1)(4) + (-3)(7) + (1)(5) + (6)(y) + (3)(-1) - ((-3)(-1) + (1)(4) + (6)(7) + (3)(5) + (-1)(y)) \right| $$
$$ Area = \frac{1}{2} \left| -4 - 21 + 5 + 6y - 3 - (3 + 4 + 42 + 15 - y) \right| $$
$$ Area = \frac{1}{2} \left| -23 + 6y - (64 - y) \right| $$
$$ Area = \frac{1}{2} \left| -23 + 6y - 64 + y \right| $$
$$ Area = \frac{1}{2} \left| 7y - 87 \right| $$
The problem states that the coordinates are $(-1, -1), (-3, 4), (1, 7), (6, 5)$ and $(3, -1)$. So, $y = -1$.
$$ Area = \frac{1}{2} |7(-1) - 87| = \frac{1}{2} |-7 - 87| = \frac{1}{2} |-94| = \frac{1}{2}(94) = 47 $$
Therefore, the area is 47.

For $E = (3, -1)$, the vertices are $A = (-1, -1), B = (-3, 4), C = (1, 7), D = (6, 5), E = (3, -1)$.
$$ Area = \frac{1}{2} |(-1)(4) + (-3)(7) + (1)(5) + (6)(-1) + (3)(-1) - ((-3)(-1) + (1)(4) + (6)(7) + (3)(5) + (-1)(-1))|$$
$$ Area = \frac{1}{2} |(-4) + (-21) + (5) + (-6) + (-3) - (3 + 4 + 42 + 15 + 1)| $$
$$ Area = \frac{1}{2} |(-4 - 21 + 5 - 6 - 3) - (3 + 4 + 42 + 15 + 1)| $$
$$ Area = \frac{1}{2} |(-29) - (65)| $$
$$ Area = \frac{1}{2} |-29 - 65| = \frac{1}{2} |-94| = \frac{1}{2} (94) = 47 $$
The area is 47 square units.

