We are given the expression $\sin^{-1}(\sin 3) + \sin^{-1}(\sin 4) + \sin^{-1}(\sin 5)$.
We want to find the exact value of this expression.

Since the range of $\sin^{-1}(x)$ is $\left[ -\frac{\pi}{2}, \frac{\pi}{2} \right]$, we need to consider the values of $3, 4, 5$ modulo $2\pi$. Since $\pi \approx 3.14$, we have $2\pi \approx 6.28$.
\begin{itemize}
    \item For the first term $\sin^{-1}(\sin 3)$, we have $3 \in \left[ -\frac{\pi}{2}, \frac{\pi}{2} \right]$. Thus, $\sin^{-1}(\sin 3) = 3$.
    \item For the second term $\sin^{-1}(\sin 4)$, we have $4 \notin \left[ -\frac{\pi}{2}, \frac{\pi}{2} \right]$. Since $4$ is in the second quadrant, we have $4 = \pi + (4 - \pi)$. We also have $4 - \pi \approx 4 - 3.14 = 0.86$, so $4 - \pi \in \left[ -\frac{\pi}{2}, \frac{\pi}{2} \right]$. Also, $\sin 4 = \sin (\pi - 4) = \sin (4-\pi)$. So $\sin^{-1}(\sin 4) = 4 - \pi$.
    \item For the third term $\sin^{-1}(\sin 5)$, we have $5 \notin \left[ -\frac{\pi}{2}, \frac{\pi}{2} \right]$. We can write $5 = 2\pi + (5 - 2\pi)$. Since $2\pi \approx 6.28$, $5 - 2\pi \approx 5 - 6.28 = -1.28$, so $5 - 2\pi \in \left[ -\frac{\pi}{2}, \frac{\pi}{2} \right]$. Also, $\sin 5 = \sin (2\pi - 5) = \sin (5-2\pi)$. So $\sin^{-1}(\sin 5) = 5 - 2\pi$.
\end{itemize}

Therefore, $\sin^{-1}(\sin 3) + \sin^{-1}(\sin 4) + \sin^{-1}(\sin 5) = 3 + (4-\pi) + (5-2\pi) = 3+4+5-\pi-2\pi = 12 - 3\pi$.

\boxed{12 - 3 \pi} 

