Let $\mathbf{v}_1 = \begin{pmatrix} 3 \\ k \\ 7 \end{pmatrix}$ and $\mathbf{v}_2 = \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 4 \\ 7 \end{pmatrix}$.
Let $\mathbf{p}_1 = \begin{pmatrix} -1 \\ -3 \\ -5 \end{pmatrix}$ be a point on the first line, and $\mathbf{p}_2 = \begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ 4 \\ 6 \end{pmatrix}$ be a point on the second line.
The two lines are coplanar if $\mathbf{v}_1 \times \mathbf{v}_2$ is orthogonal to the vector $\mathbf{p}_2 - \mathbf{p}_1$.
We have
\begin{align*} \mathbf{v}_1 \times \mathbf{v}_2 &= \begin{pmatrix} 3 \\ k \\ 7 \end{pmatrix} \times \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 4 \\ 7 \end{pmatrix} \\ &= \begin{pmatrix} (k)(7) - (7)(4) \\ (7)(1) - (3)(7) \\ (3)(4) - (k)(1) \end{pmatrix} \\ &= \begin{pmatrix} 7k - 28 \\ 7 - 21 \\ 12 - k \end{pmatrix} \\ &= \begin{pmatrix} 7k - 28 \\ -14 \\ 12 - k \end{pmatrix}. \end{align*}
Also,
\begin{align*} \mathbf{p}_2 - \mathbf{p}_1 &= \begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ 4 \\ 6 \end{pmatrix} - \begin{pmatrix} -1 \\ -3 \\ -5 \end{pmatrix} \\ &= \begin{pmatrix} 2 - (-1) \\ 4 - (-3) \\ 6 - (-5) \end{pmatrix} \\ &= \begin{pmatrix} 3 \\ 7 \\ 11 \end{pmatrix}. \end{align*}
Since the lines are coplanar, $\mathbf{v}_1 \times \mathbf{v}_2$ must be orthogonal to $\mathbf{p}_2 - \mathbf{p}_1$.
Therefore, their dot product must be zero:
\begin{align*} (\mathbf{v}_1 \times \mathbf{v}_2) \cdot (\mathbf{p}_2 - \mathbf{p}_1) &= \begin{pmatrix} 7k - 28 \\ -14 \\ 12 - k \end{pmatrix} \cdot \begin{pmatrix} 3 \\ 7 \\ 11 \end{pmatrix} \\ &= (7k - 28)(3) + (-14)(7) + (12 - k)(11) \\ &= 21k - 84 - 98 + 132 - 11k \\ &= 10k - 49 + 132 \\ &= 10k + 49 \\ &= 0. \end{align*}
So $10k = -49$, which means $k = - \frac{49}{10}$.
Thus, $k = -\frac{49}{10}$.

\boxed{-49/10}

