Let $v = \begin{pmatrix} x \\ y \end{pmatrix}$ be a vector in $\mathbb{R}^2$. We want to find the matrix $P$ such that $Pv$ is the projection of $v$ onto the $y$-axis. The projection of $v$ onto the $y$-axis is given by $\begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ y \end{pmatrix}$.
Let $P = \begin{pmatrix} a & b \\ c & d \end{pmatrix}$. Then
$$Pv = \begin{pmatrix} a & b \\ c & d \end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix} x \\ y \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} ax+by \\ cx+dy \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ y \end{pmatrix}.$$
Comparing the entries, we have
\begin{align*} ax+by &= 0 \\ cx+dy &= y \end{align*}
Since the first equation must hold for all $x$ and $y$, we must have $a=0$ and $b=0$.
The second equation becomes $cx+dy = y$. This must hold for all $x$ and $y$, so we must have $c=0$ and $d=1$.
Therefore, the projection matrix is $P = \begin{pmatrix} 0 & 0 \\ 0 & 1 \end{pmatrix}$.

\boxed{ \begin{pmatrix} 0 & 0 \\ 0 & 1 \end{pmatrix} }
\boxed{ \begin{pmatrix} 0 & 0 \\ 0 & 1 \end{pmatrix} }

