We will use synthetic division to divide $x^4 - 3x^3 + 4x^2 + 11x - 9$ by $x^2 - 3x + 2$. First, we factor the divisor: $x^2 - 3x + 2 = (x-1)(x-2)$.

Now we perform synthetic division twice, once with $x=1$ and once with $x=2$.

For $x=1$:
\begin{array}{c|ccccc}
1 & 1 & -3 & 4 & 11 & -9 \\
& & 1 & -2 & 2 & 13 \\
\hline
& 1 & -2 & 2 & 13 & 4
\end{array}
The remainder is 4.

For $x=2$:
\begin{array}{c|ccccc}
2 & 1 & -3 & 4 & 11 & -9 \\
& & 2 & -2 & 4 & 30 \\
\hline
& 1 & -1 & 2 & 15 & 21
\end{array}
The remainder is 21.

Alternatively, we can perform polynomial long division.
\begin{array}{c|ccccc}
\multicolumn{2}{r}{x^2} & & +2 \\
\cline{2-6}
x^2-3x+2 & x^4 & -3x^3 & +4x^2 & +11x & -9 \\
\multicolumn{2}{r}{x^4} & -3x^3 & +2x^2 \\
\cline{2-4}
\multicolumn{2}{r}{0} & 0 & 2x^2 & +11x & -9 \\
\multicolumn{2}{r}{} & & 2x^2 & -6x & +4 \\
\cline{4-6}
\multicolumn{2}{r}{} & & 0 & 17x & -13 \\
